    "html": "\n<p><a class=\"txi-back\" href=\"../blog.html\">&larr; Back to all articles</a></p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../assets/img/conference-panel.webp\" alt=\"Diversifying a Trading Portfolio: Beyond 'Don't Put All Eggs in One Basket'\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1600\" height=\"1066\">\n<figcaption>Trading Expo India 2027 &mdash; 23&ndash;24 April 2027, India.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p>'Diversify' is the most given and least understood advice in trading. Most traders think it means holding many positions — then discover all twenty of their stocks fell together in a market selloff. That is not diversification; it is diversification theatre.</p>\n<p>Real diversification means spreading exposure across uncorrelated return drivers. Here is how traders should think about it.</p>\n<h2>The correlation trap</h2>\n<p>Ten banking stocks are one bet on financials, however different the company names. True diversification asks: what makes each position money, and do those drivers fail together? Positions driven by the same factor — interest rates, crude oil, Nifty direction — will correlate precisely when diversification matters most: in a crisis.</p>\n<p>Audit your book by driver, not by ticker. If everything needs 'risk-on global sentiment' to work, you are concentrated no matter how many lines your portfolio statement has.</p>\n<h2>Diversify across dimensions</h2>\n<p>Think in four dimensions: asset classes (equities, currency derivatives like USD–INR, commodities), strategies (trend-following plus mean-reversion behave differently), timeframes (intraday, swing and positional positions react to different catalysts), and geographies (domestic plus international exposure where permitted).</p>\n<p>You do not need all four on day one. Adding one genuinely uncorrelated return stream does more for your risk-adjusted returns than adding ten correlated ones.</p>\n<h2>Position-level diversification rules</h2>\n<p>Practical rules: cap single-position risk at 1–2% of capital, cap sector/theme exposure (e.g., no more than 15–20% of risk in one theme), and limit total portfolio heat to 5–6% as covered in our <a href=\"risk-management-trading-complete-guide.html\">risk management guide</a>. Rebalance when winners grow into oversized concentrations — the best-performing position often becomes your biggest risk.</p>\n<p>Diversification is not a one-time setup; it is maintenance. Review concentrations monthly.</p>\n<h2>Rebalancing: the maintenance habit that protects diversification</h2>\n<p>Diversification decays without maintenance: winners grow into oversized concentrations, correlations spike during crises, and a carefully balanced book drifts into a concentrated one within months. Rebalancing is the scheduled correction — quarterly for most traders — where you trim positions that have grown beyond their risk allocation and redeploy into underweight drivers. The discipline feels unnatural (selling winners, buying laggards) but it systematically enforces 'buy low, sell high' at the portfolio level and prevents any single thesis from dominating your outcomes. Set calendar reminders; do not rely on noticing.</p>\n<p>Rebalance risk, not just capital: a position that doubled may now represent triple its original risk share even if it is the same percentage of a grown portfolio — compute heat per position, not just weight. Also use rebalancing reviews to check correlation drift: the two 'uncorrelated' positions you added last year may now move together as macro regimes shifted. True diversification is a moving target because correlations themselves move. The traders who maintain genuinely diversified books share one trait: they treat diversification as a verb — an ongoing practice of measuring, trimming and adjusting — rather than a noun describing a portfolio they built once. Start this quarter: list every position's true return driver in a spreadsheet and compute your actual heat — the results usually surprise.</p>\n<h2>Multi-asset thinking at the expo</h2>\n<p>Explore asset classes beyond your comfort zone at <a href=\"../index.html\">Trading Expo India 2027</a> (23–24 April 2027): currency, commodity and equity specialists exhibit side by side across 70–80 booths.</p>\n<p>Walk the <a href=\"../exhibit.html\">floor</a>, compare opportunities, and <a href=\"../tickets.html\">book your pass</a>.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<div class=\"txi-faq\">\n<details><summary>How many stocks should a diversified trader hold?</summary><p>Count risk drivers, not tickers. Five positions across uncorrelated drivers diversify better than twenty stocks in two sectors. For most traders, 4–8 genuinely different exposures is a practical range.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Does diversification reduce returns?</summary><p>It reduces volatility and tail risk; it can modestly reduce peak returns in raging bull markets. The trade is intentional: smoother compounding and survival through regimes that destroy concentrated books.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Can I diversify with Indian currency derivatives?</summary><p>Yes — USD–INR and other INR pairs respond to macro drivers (RBI policy, crude, global dollar) that differ from equity drivers, making them a useful diversification leg. See our USD–INR guide.</p></details>\n</div>\n<div class=\"txi-author\">\n<h3>About the author</h3>\n<p>Written by the <strong>Trading Expo India editorial team</strong> &mdash; market researchers and event producers behind India&rsquo;s premier trading &amp; fintech exhibition, 23&ndash;24 April 2027. We publish practical, hype-free guides for Indian traders. <a href=\"../contact.html\">Contact us</a>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"txi-related\">\n<h3>Related articles</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"risk-management-trading-complete-guide.html\">Risk Management in Trading: The Complete Guide (2026)</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"position-sizing-trading-guide.html\">Position Sizing in Trading: The Math That Matters More Than Strategy</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"usdinr-currency-futures-india.html\">USD–INR Currency Futures in India: A Beginner's Guide to Trading the Rupee</a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"txi-cta-box\">\n<h3>Meet the market in person.</h3>\n<p>Join 10,000+ visitors at Trading Expo India 2027, 23&ndash;24 April 2027. Early-bird passes from &#8377;249.</p>\n<p><a class=\"btn btn-primary btn-lg\" href=\"../tickets.html\">Book your ticket</a></p>\n</div>\n<p><a class=\"txi-back\" href=\"../blog.html\">&larr; Back to all articles</a></p>\n"
