    "html": "\n<p><a class=\"txi-back\" href=\"../blog.html\">&larr; Back to all articles</a></p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../assets/img/expo-registration.webp\" alt=\"Is Cryptocurrency Legal in India? The Honest 2026 Answer\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1920\" height=\"1280\">\n<figcaption>Trading Expo India 2027 &mdash; 23&ndash;24 April 2027, India.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p>'Is crypto legal in India?' remains one of the most searched finance questions in the country, and the confusion is understandable: the government taxes it at 30%, requires exchanges to register with FIU-IND, and has explored restrictions — all at once.</p>\n<p>The honest 2026 answer has three parts: legality, regulation and recognition. They are different things.</p>\n<h2>Legal: yes, with conditions</h2>\n<p>Holding and trading cryptocurrencies is not illegal in India. The Supreme Court's 2020 decision lifting the RBI's banking ban reopened the industry, and subsequent frameworks — VDA taxation, FIU-IND registration — implicitly acknowledge crypto activity as a lawful, taxable economic activity.</p>\n<p>What remains restricted are specific misuses: using crypto to evade taxes, launder money or bypass foreign-exchange controls will attract enforcement. Legality protects the honest participant, not the scheme.</p>\n<h2>Regulated: partially, and evolving</h2>\n<p>Crypto in India sits in a hybrid zone: taxed as VDAs, exchanges registered under PMLA via FIU-IND, advertising guidelines in force — but no comprehensive crypto-specific regulator or licensing regime equivalent to SEBI's role in securities. Policy continues to evolve, with the government weighing global coordination through forums like the G20.</p>\n<p>For traders, the practical rule is to watch official announcements rather than rumours. Regulatory shifts move markets; the informed trader reads the gazette, not the group chat.</p>\n<h2>Recognised: no — not legal tender</h2>\n<p>Cryptocurrency is not legal tender in India: no one is obliged to accept it as payment, and the RBI has consistently distinguished private cryptocurrencies from its own digital currency, the e-rupee (CBDC). This distinction matters for anyone building a business around crypto payments.</p>\n<p>Taxation at 30% does not equal endorsement — it equals revenue collection on an activity the state has chosen to permit and monitor. Trade accordingly: compliantly, transparently and with records.</p>\n<h2>Banking and crypto: the on-ramp reality in 2026</h2>\n<p>Legality on paper meets friction in practice at the banking layer. While the Supreme Court's 2020 decision ended the formal banking ban, individual banks remain cautious: some still flag or delay crypto-related transfers, and payment processors occasionally decline transactions to exchanges. In practice, most users transact smoothly via UPI and bank transfers on FIU-registered platforms — but the occasional blocked transaction or account query is a known friction of the ecosystem. Keeping a dedicated bank account for crypto activity, separate from salary and savings, simplifies both your bookkeeping and any conversation with your bank.</p>\n<p>Peer-to-peer transfers deserve extra caution: they carry higher fraud risk, murkier TDS handling and weaker recourse if something goes wrong. For most users, the compliant exchange route — registered, KYC-verified, with automatic TDS handling — remains the sensible default despite its costs. And maintain perspective on banking access as a privilege to protect: activity that looks like structuring or layering can trigger account reviews under anti-money-laundering rules regardless of crypto's legal status. Clean, documented, explainable transactions are the way to keep your on-ramps open. Document your account's stated purpose in a short note today — it prevents a long explanation if questions ever arise later.</p>\n<h2>Get clarity from the people shaping policy</h2>\n<p>Regulatory panels at <a href=\"../index.html\">Trading Expo India 2027</a> (23–24 April 2027) will bring together voices from policy, fintech and trading to decode where Indian crypto regulation is heading.</p>\n<p>For anyone whose portfolio touches digital assets, these <a href=\"../agenda.html\">conference sessions</a> are essential listening. <a href=\"../tickets.html\">Reserve your seat</a>.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<div class=\"txi-faq\">\n<details><summary>Is Bitcoin legal in India in 2026?</summary><p>Yes — holding and trading Bitcoin is not illegal in India. It is taxed as a virtual digital asset at 30%, exchanges must register with FIU-IND, but Bitcoin is not legal tender and comprehensive regulation is still evolving.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Can the government ban crypto in India?</summary><p>Policy has moved toward regulation and taxation rather than prohibition, including global coordination efforts. Future frameworks may change the rules, so traders should follow official announcements closely.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Is crypto income taxable even if small?</summary><p>VDA gains are taxable under the 30% regime regardless of size, with 1% TDS applying above thresholds. Keep records from the first trade, not the first big one.</p></details>\n</div>\n<div class=\"txi-author\">\n<h3>About the author</h3>\n<p>Written by the <strong>Trading Expo India editorial team</strong> &mdash; market researchers and event producers behind India&rsquo;s premier trading &amp; fintech exhibition, 23&ndash;24 April 2027. We publish practical, hype-free guides for Indian traders. <a href=\"../contact.html\">Contact us</a>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"txi-related\">\n<h3>Related articles</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"crypto-tax-india-30-percent-tds.html\">Crypto Tax in India: The 30% Rule and 1% TDS Explained (2026)</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"crypto-regulation-india-fiu-registration.html\">Crypto Regulation in India: FIU Registration and What It Means for You</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"cbdcs-e-rupee-vs-crypto-india.html\">e-Rupee vs Crypto: India's CBDC Experiment Explained for Traders</a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"txi-cta-box\">\n<h3>Meet the market in person.</h3>\n<p>Join 10,000+ visitors at Trading Expo India 2027, 23&ndash;24 April 2027. Early-bird passes from &#8377;249.</p>\n<p><a class=\"btn btn-primary btn-lg\" href=\"../tickets.html\">Book your ticket</a></p>\n</div>\n<p><a class=\"txi-back\" href=\"../blog.html\">&larr; Back to all articles</a></p>\n"
