    "html": "\n<p><a class=\"txi-back\" href=\"../blog.html\">&larr; Back to all articles</a></p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../assets/img/expo-floor-aerial.webp\" alt=\"Trading Psychology: Mastering Fear, Greed and Revenge Trading\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1920\" height=\"1280\">\n<figcaption>Trading Expo India 2027 &mdash; 23&ndash;24 April 2027, India.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p>Every trader knows the theory: cut losses, let winners run, follow the plan. And every trader has watched themselves do the opposite in real time — freezing on a stop-loss, revenge-trading after a loss, euphorically oversizing after wins. Trading psychology is the study of that gap between knowledge and behaviour.</p>\n<p>Here are the four emotional patterns that destroy accounts, and concrete systems to neutralise each.</p>\n<h2>Revenge trading: the account killer</h2>\n<p>After a loss, the brain demands immediate repair — so traders jump back in with bigger size and worse setups, converting a normal 1R loss into a 5R catastrophe. The pattern is so consistent it has a name and a near-universal outcome.</p>\n<p>The system that defeats it: a hard daily stop-loss (e.g., −2% of capital) that ends all trading for the day, no exceptions. Not a guideline — a circuit breaker. Walk away, exercise, return tomorrow. The market will still be there.</p>\n<h2>FOMO: buying the top of other people's profits</h2>\n<p>FOMO — the fear of missing out — strikes when you see a vertical move without you. You enter late, at the worst price, just as early buyers distribute into your euphoria. Social media amplifies it enormously: you see only the winners' screenshots, never their blown accounts.</p>\n<p>The antidote is a written rule: no entries more than X% extended from your planned entry zone, ever. Missed moves are tuition-free lessons; chased moves are expensive ones.</p>\n<h2>Loss aversion and the disposition effect</h2>\n<p>Behavioural finance shows losses hurt roughly twice as much as equivalent gains please — so traders sell winners too early (to 'lock in' relief) and hold losers too long (to avoid realising pain). This inverts the required asymmetry of profitable trading.</p>\n<p>Counter it mechanically: set stop-loss and target orders at entry, when you are rational, and let them execute without interference. Judge decisions by process quality at the time, not by outcome — good decisions lose money sometimes.</p>\n<h2>Building a pre-trade routine that enforces discipline</h2>\n<p>Elite performers in every field use routines to channel arousal into execution — traders need them more than most. Design a pre-trade checklist you complete before every single entry: one, is this setup on my approved list? Two, is the risk 1% or less with the stop at a structural invalidation point? Three, what is the R-multiple of the target, and is it 1.5 or better? Four, am I within my daily loss limit and portfolio heat cap? Five, what is my emotional state on a 1–5 scale — and if it is 4 or above (agitated, euphoric, desperate), do I stand down? Any 'no' aborts the trade. The checklist takes sixty seconds and blocks the majority of impulsive entries.</p>\n<p>Extend the routine to session structure: define your trading window in advance (e.g., first two hours only for intraday), schedule breaks away from screens, and end each session with a two-minute log — trades taken, plan deviations, emotional notes. Over weeks, this routine builds something willpower cannot: identity. You stop being someone trying to be disciplined and become someone who follows a process. That identity shift — from outcome-chasing to process-executing — is the psychological destination all these techniques serve. Build the routine, trust the routine, become the routine.</p>\n<h2>Build the mental game deliberately</h2>\n<p>Elite performance psychology — routines, reviews, accountability — applies to trading as much as to sport. Consider working through these frameworks with mentors who have survived multiple market cycles.</p>\n<p>Meet experienced traders and performance coaches at <a href=\"../index.html\">Trading Expo India 2027</a> (23–24 April 2027): psychology and discipline sessions run across the <a href=\"../agenda.html\">agenda</a>. <a href=\"../tickets.html\">Book your pass</a>.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<div class=\"txi-faq\">\n<details><summary>How do I stop revenge trading?</summary><p>Install a non-negotiable daily loss limit (e.g., 2% of capital) that ends your trading day when hit. Remove discretion from the decision — pre-commitment beats willpower every time.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Why do I sell winners early but hold losers?</summary><p>Loss aversion: the pain of realising a loss exceeds the pleasure of banking a gain, so you avoid the pain and grab the relief. Mechanical stop/target orders placed at entry bypass this bias.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Can trading psychology be learned?</summary><p>Yes — through systems, not pep talks. Pre-trade checklists, hard risk rules, journaling and structured reviews convert emotional impulses into manageable data over time.</p></details>\n</div>\n<div class=\"txi-author\">\n<h3>About the author</h3>\n<p>Written by the <strong>Trading Expo India editorial team</strong> &mdash; market researchers and event producers behind India&rsquo;s premier trading &amp; fintech exhibition, 23&ndash;24 April 2027. We publish practical, hype-free guides for Indian traders. <a href=\"../contact.html\">Contact us</a>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"txi-related\">\n<h3>Related articles</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"risk-management-trading-complete-guide.html\">Risk Management in Trading: The Complete Guide (2026)</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"trading-journal-guide-improve-results.html\">Trading Journal Guide: How Documenting Trades Transforms Results</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"intraday-trading-strategy-guide.html\">Intraday Trading Strategy Guide: What Actually Works (and What Doesn't)</a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"txi-cta-box\">\n<h3>Meet the market in person.</h3>\n<p>Join 10,000+ visitors at Trading Expo India 2027, 23&ndash;24 April 2027. Early-bird passes from &#8377;249.</p>\n<p><a class=\"btn btn-primary btn-lg\" href=\"../tickets.html\">Book your ticket</a></p>\n</div>\n<p><a class=\"txi-back\" href=\"../blog.html\">&larr; Back to all articles</a></p>\n"
